1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,775
You ask a question, and a moment later the answer starts streaming back, one

2
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:06,338
token at a time.

3
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:10,500
The model writing it is the result of one of the largest computations people

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:13,125
have ever run.

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:18,125
Here is every notable AI model with a published estimate of its training compute,

6
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:22,725
placed by release date and by how many arithmetic operations it took to train,

7
00:00:22,725 --> 00:00:24,650
on a logarithmic scale.

8
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:26,838
GPT-2, in 2019:

9
00:00:26,838 --> 00:00:29,425
about ten to the twenty-one operations.

10
00:00:29,425 --> 00:00:31,438
GPT-3, a year later:

11
00:00:31,438 --> 00:00:33,250
more than a hundred times that.

12
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:34,488
GPT-4:

13
00:00:34,488 --> 00:00:37,025
about seventy times more again.

14
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:43,750
The largest runs of 2025 and 2026 are estimated at ten to the twenty-six operations

15
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:45,075
and beyond.

16
00:00:45,075 --> 00:00:49,838
At the frontier, training compute has been growing almost five times, every

17
00:00:49,838 --> 00:00:51,525
single year.

18
00:00:51,525 --> 00:00:53,813
And so that none of this is a cartoon:

19
00:00:53,813 --> 00:00:58,450
every experiment in this video was actually run, in miniature, on the four-core

20
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:00,338
computer that rendered it.

21
00:01:00,338 --> 00:01:04,762
All of our training runs together took less than ten to the fifteen operations,

22
00:01:04,762 --> 00:01:08,350
roughly a hundred billion times less than a frontier run.

23
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:10,062
The scale is different.

24
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:13,200
The ideas are the same.

25
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:17,250
This video follows one of those training runs from end to end.

26
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:21,588
It starts with a crawl of the public web, which is filtered, deduplicated

27
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:24,963
and mixed into a dataset of tens of trillions of tokens.

28
00:01:24,963 --> 00:01:28,863
Pretraining teaches a network to predict the next token of that text, with

29
00:01:28,863 --> 00:01:32,563
the work spread across tens of thousands of GPUs for months.

30
00:01:32,563 --> 00:01:36,425
Post-training then turns that raw text predictor into something that follows

31
00:01:36,425 --> 00:01:41,600
instructions and reasons, and the result is the assistant.

32
00:01:42,242 --> 00:01:43,842
We'll go in four parts:

33
00:01:43,842 --> 00:01:49,117
the data, the training recipe, scaling it out across a cluster, and post-training.

34
00:01:49,117 --> 00:01:52,667
Along the way, we'll look at what the labs have actually published about how

35
00:01:52,667 --> 00:01:55,592
they do it, as of October 2026.

36
00:02:01,073 --> 00:02:01,773
Part one:

37
00:02:01,773 --> 00:02:04,023
the data.

38
00:02:05,048 --> 00:02:08,423
Almost every large language model starts from the same place:

39
00:02:08,423 --> 00:02:12,311
Common Crawl, a non-profit that crawls the web every month or two and gives

40
00:02:12,311 --> 00:02:13,723
the result away.

41
00:02:13,723 --> 00:02:19,223
Its September 2026 crawl alone holds 2.17 billion web pages, a hundred and

42
00:02:19,223 --> 00:02:23,211
six terabytes compressed, split into a hundred thousand files.

43
00:02:23,211 --> 00:02:25,248
Each square here is one of them.

44
00:02:25,248 --> 00:02:29,686
For this video, we downloaded eight of them, plus the first four hundred megabytes

45
00:02:29,686 --> 00:02:33,598
of the raw HTML behind the first.

46
00:02:34,023 --> 00:02:36,611
Here's one page from the very first file:

47
00:02:36,611 --> 00:02:41,073
a blog post with a recipe for savory custard.

48
00:02:41,248 --> 00:02:43,311
This is what the crawler actually stored:

49
00:02:43,311 --> 00:02:46,273
more than two thousand lines of HTML.

50
00:02:46,273 --> 00:02:49,798
Scripts, style sheets, menus, links, metadata.

51
00:02:49,798 --> 00:02:51,461
And the recipe itself?

52
00:02:51,461 --> 00:02:53,386
All of it sits on a single line:

53
00:02:53,386 --> 00:02:55,548
number 436.

54
00:02:55,548 --> 00:02:59,523
Ninety-eight kilobytes of HTML, for three kilobytes of text:

55
00:02:59,523 --> 00:03:02,298
about three percent.

56
00:03:02,856 --> 00:03:06,919
Across the first ten thousand pages of that file, it's the same story:

57
00:03:06,919 --> 00:03:11,794
1.65 gigabytes of HTML become thirty-seven megabytes of text.

58
00:03:11,794 --> 00:03:14,594
Pulling out the main text is a step of its own.

59
00:03:14,594 --> 00:03:19,356
Common Crawl ships an extracted version, but the FineWeb team found that re-extracting

60
00:03:19,356 --> 00:03:24,331
from the raw HTML, with a library called Trafilatura, leaves out more menus

61
00:03:24,331 --> 00:03:27,156
and boilerplate, and trains better models.

62
00:03:27,156 --> 00:03:29,881
Here's our recipe page both ways.

63
00:03:29,881 --> 00:03:34,081
Common Crawl's version keeps the comments, the cookbook ads, and a hundred-line

64
00:03:34,081 --> 00:03:35,419
blog archive.

65
00:03:35,419 --> 00:03:39,206
Over all the pages that survive our filters, it's twice as long.

66
00:03:39,206 --> 00:03:41,119
And the web is multilingual:

67
00:03:41,119 --> 00:03:44,956
in this sample, only four pages in ten are in English.

68
00:03:44,956 --> 00:03:47,444
Frontier models train on many languages;

69
00:03:47,444 --> 00:03:50,581
here, like the original FineWeb, we'll keep English.

70
00:03:52,688 --> 00:03:54,188
Next, filtering.

71
00:03:54,188 --> 00:03:59,026
We took the exact recipe behind FineWeb, Hugging Face's open web dataset of

72
00:03:59,026 --> 00:04:04,076
fifteen trillion tokens, and ran their own code on those ten thousand pages.

73
00:04:04,076 --> 00:04:07,213
Here's how many pages survive each step.

74
00:04:07,213 --> 00:04:11,551
First, a blocklist of four and a half million domains removes adult sites,

75
00:04:11,551 --> 00:04:13,326
malware and spam.

76
00:04:13,326 --> 00:04:17,988
Pages with no extractable text go next, like this one, which does nothing

77
00:04:17,988 --> 00:04:19,888
but redirect your browser.

78
00:04:19,888 --> 00:04:24,226
Then a language classifier keeps only pages that are confidently English:

79
00:04:24,226 --> 00:04:26,471
about a third of what's left.

80
00:04:26,471 --> 00:04:28,684
Then come rules written by hand.

81
00:04:28,684 --> 00:04:33,046
Repetition rules, from DeepMind's Gopher paper, drop pages where too many

82
00:04:33,046 --> 00:04:37,496
lines, paragraphs or phrases repeat, like this forum index:

83
00:04:37,496 --> 00:04:42,521
zero replies, last post by Archive, over and over.

84
00:04:42,521 --> 00:04:47,146
Quality rules drop pages with fewer than fifty words, or where too few words

85
00:04:47,146 --> 00:04:51,259
contain a letter, or that lack ordinary words like the and and.

86
00:04:51,259 --> 00:04:54,071
Like this login form.

87
00:04:54,071 --> 00:04:59,246
Rules from Google's C4 dataset drop lines that don't end in punctuation, and

88
00:04:59,246 --> 00:05:02,659
any page with lorem ipsum or a curly bracket in it:

89
00:05:02,659 --> 00:05:04,959
a sign of code or templates.

90
00:05:04,959 --> 00:05:10,971
This page is spun advertising text with its template syntax still showing.

91
00:05:10,971 --> 00:05:15,259
And FineWeb added three rules of its own, chosen by training small models

92
00:05:15,259 --> 00:05:16,434
on the output:

93
00:05:16,434 --> 00:05:21,509
drop pages where few lines end in punctuation, where most lines are very short,

94
00:05:21,509 --> 00:05:23,571
or where many lines repeat.

95
00:05:23,571 --> 00:05:27,296
Menus and navigation, mostly.

96
00:05:27,471 --> 00:05:28,909
These rules are blunt.

97
00:05:28,909 --> 00:05:32,371
This page, a programming puzzle full of code, was thrown out.

98
00:05:32,371 --> 00:05:33,946
Here is the arithmetic.

99
00:05:33,946 --> 00:05:36,321
It passes six of Gopher's seven tests:

100
00:05:36,321 --> 00:05:40,521
enough words, a normal word length, few hashes, bullets or trailing dots,

101
00:05:40,521 --> 00:05:43,571
and plenty of ordinary words like the, and, of.

102
00:05:43,571 --> 00:05:49,271
But of its 2,756 words, only 2,037 contain a letter:

103
00:05:49,271 --> 00:05:53,421
73.9 percent, under the 80 percent threshold.

104
00:05:53,421 --> 00:05:57,121
The rest are quotes, brackets and numbers from its code.

105
00:05:57,121 --> 00:05:59,759
At web scale that's an acceptable trade:

106
00:05:59,759 --> 00:06:05,046
losing some good pages costs far less than training on bad ones.

107
00:06:05,746 --> 00:06:09,884
In the end, thirteen percent of the pages survive, holding about a third of

108
00:06:09,884 --> 00:06:12,096
a percent of the original bytes.

109
00:06:12,096 --> 00:06:17,234
FineWeb ran this, plus deduplication, over ninety-six crawls, and ended up

110
00:06:17,234 --> 00:06:19,196
with fifteen trillion tokens.

111
00:06:21,235 --> 00:06:23,048
The web is full of copies:

112
00:06:23,048 --> 00:06:26,423
mirrored sites, templates, boilerplate pages.

113
00:06:26,423 --> 00:06:31,723
Training on the same text over and over wastes compute and encourages memorization.

114
00:06:31,723 --> 00:06:35,223
Here's the biggest cluster of near-duplicates in our eight files:

115
00:06:35,223 --> 00:06:40,560
forty-one pages announcing that some domain name is for sale.

116
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:45,785
To measure how alike two pages are, cut each one into overlapping five-word

117
00:06:45,785 --> 00:06:46,823
shingles.

118
00:06:46,823 --> 00:06:50,785
The Jaccard similarity is the number of shingles they share, divided by the

119
00:06:50,785 --> 00:06:53,123
number of distinct shingles in either.

120
00:06:53,123 --> 00:06:57,760
For these two pages, it's 0.78.

121
00:06:58,165 --> 00:07:01,365
But comparing every page with every other is hopeless.

122
00:07:01,365 --> 00:07:04,965
Billions of pages means billions of billions of pairs.

123
00:07:04,965 --> 00:07:07,852
MinHash gets around this with a beautiful trick.

124
00:07:07,852 --> 00:07:11,990
Run every shingle of a page through a random hash function, and keep only

125
00:07:11,990 --> 00:07:13,790
the smallest value.

126
00:07:13,790 --> 00:07:17,727
Two pages get the same minimum exactly when the smallest-hashed shingle of

127
00:07:17,727 --> 00:07:22,390
the two pages combined is one they share, which happens with probability equal

128
00:07:22,390 --> 00:07:24,590
to their Jaccard similarity.

129
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:30,127
Repeat with 112 different hash functions, and each page gets a short fingerprint.

130
00:07:30,127 --> 00:07:34,352
For our two pages, 86 of the 112 minimums agree:

131
00:07:34,352 --> 00:07:38,440
an estimate of 0.77.

132
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:42,827
Then, instead of comparing fingerprints, hash them.

133
00:07:42,827 --> 00:07:46,302
Cut the 112 numbers into 14 bands of eight.

134
00:07:46,302 --> 00:07:50,265
Pages that match on all eight numbers of any band land in the same bucket,

135
00:07:50,265 --> 00:07:52,002
and become candidates.

136
00:07:52,002 --> 00:07:56,002
The chance of that, as a function of similarity, is this S-curve:

137
00:07:56,002 --> 00:07:59,952
pages that are eighty percent alike are caught ninety-two percent of the time;

138
00:07:59,952 --> 00:08:04,390
pages that are half alike, about five percent.

139
00:08:04,885 --> 00:08:08,685
In our eight files, deduplication removed about three percent of the pages

140
00:08:08,685 --> 00:08:12,085
that survived filtering, in 114 clusters:

141
00:08:12,085 --> 00:08:16,948
domain-for-sale pages, access-denied pages, default web-hosting pages.

142
00:08:16,948 --> 00:08:21,035
Across a hundred crawls, the same pages turn up again and again, so the stakes

143
00:08:21,035 --> 00:08:23,948
are much higher, and FineWeb found a surprise.

144
00:08:23,948 --> 00:08:27,635
Deduplicating across all the crawls threw away ninety percent of the oldest

145
00:08:27,635 --> 00:08:30,723
ones, and what remained trained worse models.

146
00:08:30,723 --> 00:08:33,735
So FineWeb deduplicates each crawl separately.

147
00:08:35,667 --> 00:08:39,492
Hand-written rules catch obvious junk, but they can't tell whether a page

148
00:08:39,492 --> 00:08:41,067
is worth learning from.

149
00:08:41,067 --> 00:08:43,605
For that, labs use models.

150
00:08:43,605 --> 00:08:48,567
FineWeb-Edu asked Llama 3 70B to grade half a million pages for educational

151
00:08:48,567 --> 00:08:53,467
value, from zero to five, trained a small, fast classifier to imitate those

152
00:08:53,467 --> 00:08:57,480
grades, and ran it over all fifteen trillion tokens.

153
00:08:57,480 --> 00:09:01,492
Then it kept the pages scoring three or more.

154
00:09:01,963 --> 00:09:06,038
We ran that same classifier on four hundred random pages that survived our

155
00:09:06,038 --> 00:09:07,163
filters.

156
00:09:07,163 --> 00:09:08,438
Most score low:

157
00:09:08,438 --> 00:09:10,901
shops, services, local news.

158
00:09:10,901 --> 00:09:15,351
Keeping three and up, as FineWeb-Edu does, leaves just seven percent.

159
00:09:15,351 --> 00:09:20,663
The top scorer looks like this, and a typical low scorer like this.

160
00:09:21,097 --> 00:09:26,422
At full scale, FineWeb-Edu kept a similar eight percent of FineWeb, 1.3 trillion

161
00:09:26,422 --> 00:09:30,947
tokens, and models trained on it matched older datasets on knowledge benchmarks

162
00:09:30,947 --> 00:09:33,297
with ten times fewer tokens.

163
00:09:33,297 --> 00:09:37,572
Since then, the trend is to rewrite rather than throw away.

164
00:09:37,572 --> 00:09:42,384
NVIDIA's Nemotron-CC used a language model to rephrase web pages, producing

165
00:09:42,384 --> 00:09:45,572
almost two trillion synthetic tokens.

166
00:09:45,572 --> 00:09:50,722
Moonshot rephrased Kimi K2's knowledge data, and a factual-recall score rose

167
00:09:50,722 --> 00:09:53,147
from 24 to 29 percent.

168
00:09:53,147 --> 00:09:58,422
And AI2's OLMo 3 repeats its best data up to seven times, instead of drawing

169
00:09:58,422 --> 00:10:00,972
a hard line.

170
00:10:01,467 --> 00:10:03,129
Then the data is mixed.

171
00:10:03,129 --> 00:10:07,879
Llama 3's recipe was about half general knowledge, a quarter math and reasoning,

172
00:10:07,879 --> 00:10:11,492
seventeen percent code, and eight percent other languages.

173
00:10:11,492 --> 00:10:15,892
And in the last stretch of training, the mix shifts toward the very best data.

174
00:10:15,892 --> 00:10:20,567
Annealing Llama 3's 8-billion-parameter model this way raised its score on

175
00:10:20,567 --> 00:10:25,017
grade-school math problems by twenty-four percent.

176
00:10:25,458 --> 00:10:27,571
The finished datasets are enormous:

177
00:10:27,571 --> 00:10:32,871
around fifteen trillion tokens for Llama 3, DeepSeek-V3 and Kimi K2;

178
00:10:32,871 --> 00:10:35,946
twenty-eight and a half trillion for GLM-5;

179
00:10:35,946 --> 00:10:40,646
thirty-three trillion for DeepSeek-V4, released in April 2026;

180
00:10:40,646 --> 00:10:43,158
thirty-six trillion for Qwen3.

181
00:10:43,158 --> 00:10:46,083
Last, the text becomes tokens.

182
00:10:46,083 --> 00:10:51,871
A tokenizer, trained with byte-pair encoding, breaks text into frequent pieces;

183
00:10:51,871 --> 00:10:57,483
frontier tokenizers have between 128 and 260 thousand of them.

184
00:10:57,483 --> 00:11:03,958
We trained a small one, with 2,048, and used it to encode 168 million tokens

185
00:11:03,958 --> 00:11:05,458
of FineWeb-Edu:

186
00:11:05,458 --> 00:11:09,658
the fuel for the training runs in the next part.

187
00:11:10,217 --> 00:11:12,779
How does byte-pair encoding choose its pieces?

188
00:11:12,779 --> 00:11:16,942
Start from single bytes, and count every adjacent pair in the training text.

189
00:11:16,942 --> 00:11:21,179
In our 57 million words, the most common pair is a space followed by t:

190
00:11:21,179 --> 00:11:22,729
six million times.

191
00:11:22,729 --> 00:11:25,354
Merge it into one token, recount, and repeat:

192
00:11:25,354 --> 00:11:28,029
space-a, then h-e, i-n, r-e.

193
00:11:28,029 --> 00:11:31,904
At the sixth merge, space-t plus h-e makes space-the.

194
00:11:31,904 --> 00:11:36,667
Recounting the pairs ourselves gives exactly the merges our tokenizer learned.

195
00:11:36,667 --> 00:11:41,042
After two thousand merges, 'the cat sat on the mat', twenty-two bytes, becomes

196
00:11:41,042 --> 00:11:42,067
eight tokens.

197
00:11:43,941 --> 00:11:44,591
Part two:

198
00:11:44,591 --> 00:11:47,041
the recipe.

199
00:11:48,016 --> 00:11:52,228
Before training anything, a lab has to decide how big the model should be.

200
00:11:52,228 --> 00:11:55,091
The budget is compute, and there's a simple rule for it:

201
00:11:55,091 --> 00:11:58,578
training costs about six times the number of parameters, times the number

202
00:11:58,578 --> 00:11:59,816
of tokens.

203
00:11:59,816 --> 00:12:03,853
Two operations per parameter per token on the forward pass, a multiply and

204
00:12:03,853 --> 00:12:06,778
an add, and about four more on the backward pass.

205
00:12:06,778 --> 00:12:08,891
So a fixed budget is a fixed area:

206
00:12:08,891 --> 00:12:12,791
you can spend it on a big model trained on fewer tokens, or a small model

207
00:12:12,791 --> 00:12:14,103
trained on many.

208
00:12:14,103 --> 00:12:16,641
Which is better?

209
00:12:17,149 --> 00:12:19,136
Check the rule on a real model.

210
00:12:19,136 --> 00:12:24,661
Llama 3.1's largest version has 405 billion parameters, so every token costs

211
00:12:24,661 --> 00:12:25,861
six times that:

212
00:12:25,861 --> 00:12:28,224
2.4 trillion operations.

213
00:12:28,224 --> 00:12:31,411
It was trained on 15.6 trillion tokens.

214
00:12:31,411 --> 00:12:36,936
Multiply, and the whole run comes to 3.8 times ten to the twenty-five operations.

215
00:12:36,936 --> 00:12:39,924
Epoch AI's independent estimate of that run:

216
00:12:39,924 --> 00:12:44,849
the same, 3.8 times ten to the twenty-five.

217
00:12:45,382 --> 00:12:48,470
The way to find out is an experiment, so we ran one.

218
00:12:48,470 --> 00:12:52,495
Pick a compute budget, and train models of several sizes, each on exactly

219
00:12:52,495 --> 00:12:54,770
the number of tokens the budget allows.

220
00:12:54,770 --> 00:12:57,895
Plot final loss against model size, and you get a valley:

221
00:12:57,895 --> 00:13:01,332
too small a model can't hold what it sees, too large a model doesn't get to

222
00:13:01,332 --> 00:13:02,320
see enough.

223
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:06,082
Repeat at four budgets, twenty-five runs in all, and fit a parabola to each

224
00:13:06,082 --> 00:13:07,320
valley floor.

225
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:11,420
The best size grows with the budget, as a power law, with an exponent of about

226
00:13:11,420 --> 00:13:12,982
three quarters.

227
00:13:12,982 --> 00:13:17,595
That's close to what OpenAI found in 2020 with small models.

228
00:13:17,595 --> 00:13:21,745
The much larger study we'll see next found about one half, and later work

229
00:13:21,745 --> 00:13:26,220
traced the gap to details of how very small models like ours are measured

230
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:28,632
and tuned.

231
00:13:29,095 --> 00:13:33,670
In 2022, DeepMind's Chinchilla paper did this with more than four hundred

232
00:13:33,670 --> 00:13:38,257
models, up to sixteen billion parameters, and fitted a formula for the loss

233
00:13:38,257 --> 00:13:41,045
as a function of model size and data.

234
00:13:41,045 --> 00:13:44,870
Here is the landscape it describes, with the coefficients as corrected by

235
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:46,982
a 2024 replication;

236
00:13:46,982 --> 00:13:49,082
darker means lower loss.

237
00:13:49,082 --> 00:13:52,345
Each yellow line is a fixed compute budget.

238
00:13:52,345 --> 00:13:56,357
Along each one there's a best point, and together they trace this path:

239
00:13:56,357 --> 00:14:01,357
grow parameters and tokens together, about twenty tokens for every parameter.

240
00:14:01,357 --> 00:14:06,870
By that measure, GPT-3 had been trained on far too little data, and the 70-billion

241
00:14:06,870 --> 00:14:12,170
parameter Chinchilla, trained on 1.4 trillion tokens, beat a model four times

242
00:14:12,170 --> 00:14:14,545
its size.

243
00:14:14,986 --> 00:14:17,924
The formula also tells you how to split any budget.

244
00:14:17,924 --> 00:14:22,799
Since C is six N D, the number of tokens is C over six N, so the loss becomes

245
00:14:22,799 --> 00:14:25,211
a function of the model size alone.

246
00:14:25,211 --> 00:14:29,136
Set its derivative to zero, and the best size turns out to be a power of the

247
00:14:29,136 --> 00:14:29,986
budget:

248
00:14:29,986 --> 00:14:32,736
C to the beta over alpha plus beta.

249
00:14:32,736 --> 00:14:38,386
With the corrected coefficients, that exponent is 0.51, so model size and

250
00:14:38,386 --> 00:14:41,261
data should grow at almost the same rate.

251
00:14:41,261 --> 00:14:47,674
Now plug in Chinchilla's own budget, 5.76 times ten to the twenty-three operations.

252
00:14:47,674 --> 00:14:53,474
The formula asks for 72 billion parameters and 1.3 trillion tokens, about

253
00:14:53,474 --> 00:14:55,861
eighteen tokens per parameter.

254
00:14:55,861 --> 00:15:02,961
DeepMind trained Chinchilla with 70 billion parameters on 1.4 trillion tokens.

255
00:15:03,503 --> 00:15:06,003
But nobody actually stops at twenty.

256
00:15:06,003 --> 00:15:10,503
Llama 3's team used the same kind of IsoFLOP experiment, up to ten to the

257
00:15:10,503 --> 00:15:16,228
twenty-two operations, to choose the size of their 405-billion-parameter model,

258
00:15:16,228 --> 00:15:19,141
and then trained their small models far longer:

259
00:15:19,141 --> 00:15:24,378
the 8-billion-parameter Llama 3 saw fifteen trillion tokens, almost nineteen

260
00:15:24,378 --> 00:15:27,653
hundred per parameter, and it kept improving.

261
00:15:27,653 --> 00:15:30,016
The reason is cost after training:

262
00:15:30,016 --> 00:15:33,978
a smaller model trained for longer is much cheaper to run for millions of

263
00:15:33,978 --> 00:15:35,028
people.

264
00:15:35,028 --> 00:15:39,603
Frontier mixtures of experts land in between, at hundreds to over a thousand

265
00:15:39,603 --> 00:15:41,703
tokens per active parameter.

266
00:15:43,572 --> 00:15:44,634
What gets trained?

267
00:15:44,634 --> 00:15:45,997
Still a transformer:

268
00:15:45,997 --> 00:15:50,272
the same basic blueprint as GPT-2, which our earlier video took apart piece

269
00:15:50,272 --> 00:15:51,347
by piece.

270
00:15:51,347 --> 00:15:53,834
But nearly every component has been refined:

271
00:15:53,834 --> 00:15:58,972
a simpler normalization, rotary position embeddings, gated MLPs, attention

272
00:15:58,972 --> 00:16:03,559
that shares or compresses its keys and values to save memory, and normalization

273
00:16:03,559 --> 00:16:05,997
of the queries and keys for stability.

274
00:16:05,997 --> 00:16:10,284
Our pocket models use the first three, and QK-norm gets an experiment of its

275
00:16:10,284 --> 00:16:12,897
own later on.

276
00:16:13,330 --> 00:16:17,717
The biggest architectural change of the last two years is the mixture of experts.

277
00:16:17,717 --> 00:16:22,155
In a standard transformer, every token passes through the whole MLP in every

278
00:16:22,155 --> 00:16:23,217
layer.

279
00:16:23,217 --> 00:16:27,880
A mixture-of-experts layer replaces that one MLP with many smaller ones, the

280
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:32,180
experts, and a small router that scores them for each token and sends the

281
00:16:32,180 --> 00:16:34,267
token to only the top few.

282
00:16:34,267 --> 00:16:38,330
So the number of parameters grows with the number of experts, while the computation

283
00:16:38,330 --> 00:16:42,555
per token grows only with the number chosen.

284
00:16:43,044 --> 00:16:44,044
In symbols:

285
00:16:44,044 --> 00:16:48,807
the router turns the token's vector into one score per expert, with a softmax.

286
00:16:48,807 --> 00:16:53,219
Keep the top two, rescale their scores to sum to one, and add up those experts'

287
00:16:53,219 --> 00:16:54,319
outputs.

288
00:16:54,319 --> 00:17:02,294
With the scores we just saw, 0.38 and 0.31 become 0.55 and 0.45.

289
00:17:02,294 --> 00:17:05,219
And the saving is plain arithmetic.

290
00:17:05,219 --> 00:17:11,994
DeepSeek-V3 has 671 billion parameters, but each token passes through 37 billion

291
00:17:11,994 --> 00:17:17,782
of them, so training costs six times 37 billion operations per token, not

292
00:17:17,782 --> 00:17:20,557
six times 671 billion:

293
00:17:20,557 --> 00:17:23,594
eighteen times less.

294
00:17:24,194 --> 00:17:26,719
Frontier open models push this hard.

295
00:17:26,719 --> 00:17:32,669
DeepSeek-V3 has 256 experts per layer and uses eight of them, plus one shared

296
00:17:32,669 --> 00:17:34,994
expert that every token visits:

297
00:17:34,994 --> 00:17:40,744
671 billion parameters in total, but only 37 billion doing work for any one

298
00:17:40,744 --> 00:17:41,857
token.

299
00:17:41,857 --> 00:17:47,719
Kimi K2 has 384 experts and passes a trillion parameters.

300
00:17:47,719 --> 00:17:55,982
And Kimi K3, from July 2026, picks sixteen of 896, for 2.8 trillion parameters.

301
00:17:55,982 --> 00:18:00,007
Experts keep getting more numerous, and the fraction used per token keeps

302
00:18:00,007 --> 00:18:04,444
shrinking, now around three to five percent.

303
00:18:04,890 --> 00:18:06,952
But routers have a failure mode.

304
00:18:06,952 --> 00:18:10,815
Experts that get more tokens improve faster and get chosen even more:

305
00:18:10,815 --> 00:18:12,177
the rich get richer.

306
00:18:12,177 --> 00:18:16,127
Here are three real runs of our pocket mixture of experts, eight experts,

307
00:18:16,127 --> 00:18:17,452
two per token.

308
00:18:17,452 --> 00:18:21,065
Watch the share of tokens each expert receives as training goes.

309
00:18:21,065 --> 00:18:25,565
With no balancing, the busiest expert ends up with three times its fair share,

310
00:18:25,565 --> 00:18:27,690
while another gets almost nothing.

311
00:18:27,690 --> 00:18:32,690
The classic fix is an extra loss term that penalizes uneven routing.

312
00:18:32,690 --> 00:18:35,302
DeepSeek-V3 used a gentler trick:

313
00:18:35,302 --> 00:18:39,865
a bias added to each expert's score, only for choosing experts, nudged up

314
00:18:39,865 --> 00:18:43,765
after every step if the expert was underused and down if it was overused,

315
00:18:43,765 --> 00:18:46,840
with no extra loss term competing with learning.

316
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:49,627
Here, it balances almost perfectly.

317
00:18:49,627 --> 00:18:53,640
In our tiny runs, the auxiliary loss still finished with a slightly lower

318
00:18:53,640 --> 00:18:54,427
loss;

319
00:18:54,427 --> 00:19:00,565
in DeepSeek's experiments, at billions of parameters, the bias came out ahead.

320
00:19:01,244 --> 00:19:03,869
Here's the arithmetic behind the two fixes.

321
00:19:03,869 --> 00:19:08,169
The auxiliary loss multiplies, for each expert, the share of tokens it gets,

322
00:19:08,169 --> 00:19:12,294
f, by the average probability the router gives it, P, and adds them up.

323
00:19:12,294 --> 00:19:16,432
If every expert gets an eighth, the total is exactly one, its minimum.

324
00:19:16,432 --> 00:19:21,732
Plug in our unbalanced run's real shares, taking P close to f, and it's 1.84;

325
00:19:21,732 --> 00:19:24,894
training with the loss brought it to 1.07.

326
00:19:24,894 --> 00:19:27,994
DeepSeek's bias never touches the loss.

327
00:19:27,994 --> 00:19:33,907
After every step, each expert's bias moves by 0.001 toward balance:

328
00:19:33,907 --> 00:19:37,857
up if it got fewer tokens than average, down if it got more.

329
00:19:37,857 --> 00:19:42,344
By the end of our run, the naturally popular experts carried biases near minus

330
00:19:42,344 --> 00:19:46,557
0.3 and the neglected ones near plus 0.3:

331
00:19:46,557 --> 00:19:50,044
just enough to even out the choices.

332
00:19:50,594 --> 00:19:54,844
A few other shifts are reshaping the blueprint, mostly to make long contexts

333
00:19:54,844 --> 00:19:56,269
and inference cheaper.

334
00:19:56,269 --> 00:20:00,907
Sparse attention lets each token look at only its most relevant earlier tokens.

335
00:20:00,907 --> 00:20:04,944
Hybrid models replace three of every four attention layers with cheaper linear-time

336
00:20:04,944 --> 00:20:05,982
layers.

337
00:20:05,982 --> 00:20:10,107
Multi-token prediction adds heads that guess several tokens ahead, both as

338
00:20:10,107 --> 00:20:13,569
extra training signal and to speed up generation.

339
00:20:13,569 --> 00:20:17,994
And context windows of a million tokens are now common, reached in stages

340
00:20:17,994 --> 00:20:19,269
near the end of training.

341
00:20:21,178 --> 00:20:22,753
Next, the optimizer:

342
00:20:22,753 --> 00:20:25,640
the rule that turns each gradient into an update.

343
00:20:25,640 --> 00:20:29,990
Plain gradient descent steps straight downhill, and in a long, narrow valley

344
00:20:29,990 --> 00:20:33,903
it zigzags across the steep walls while creeping along the floor.

345
00:20:33,903 --> 00:20:36,740
Adam fixes this one coordinate at a time:

346
00:20:36,740 --> 00:20:40,903
it keeps running averages of each parameter's gradient and squared gradient,

347
00:20:40,903 --> 00:20:44,940
and divides one by the square root of the other, so every coordinate moves

348
00:20:44,940 --> 00:20:46,803
at a similar speed.

349
00:20:46,803 --> 00:20:53,178
With decoupled weight decay, AdamW has been the default for most of a decade.

350
00:20:53,661 --> 00:20:55,411
Here's Adam in equations.

351
00:20:55,411 --> 00:20:59,448
It keeps a running average of the gradient, m, and of the squared gradient,

352
00:20:59,448 --> 00:20:59,636
v.

353
00:20:59,636 --> 00:21:03,386
The step is m divided by the square root of v, after a correction for the

354
00:21:03,386 --> 00:21:05,336
averages starting at zero.

355
00:21:05,336 --> 00:21:06,986
Run it on two parameters:

356
00:21:06,986 --> 00:21:11,361
one with gradients around four, one with gradients a hundred times smaller.

357
00:21:11,361 --> 00:21:15,498
The averages differ by a factor of a hundred, but their ratio doesn't:

358
00:21:15,498 --> 00:21:20,911
after three steps both parameters move by 0.974 learning rates.

359
00:21:20,911 --> 00:21:25,773
AdamW adds one more term, shrinking every weight slightly, kept separate from

360
00:21:25,773 --> 00:21:28,361
the gradient step.

361
00:21:28,936 --> 00:21:33,123
A newer optimizer, Muon, treats each weight matrix as a whole.

362
00:21:33,123 --> 00:21:37,498
A matrix's gradient can be broken into directions, its singular vectors, each

363
00:21:37,498 --> 00:21:38,723
with a size.

364
00:21:38,723 --> 00:21:42,161
Here are the sizes for a real gradient from one of our pocket models, on a

365
00:21:42,161 --> 00:21:43,398
log scale.

366
00:21:43,398 --> 00:21:45,536
A handful of directions dominate:

367
00:21:45,536 --> 00:21:50,736
the top five, out of 128, carry about two thirds of it, while a typical direction

368
00:21:50,736 --> 00:21:53,836
is some fifty times smaller than the largest.

369
00:21:53,836 --> 00:21:57,323
Muon keeps the directions but sets every size to one:

370
00:21:57,323 --> 00:22:00,836
each direction gets an equal step.

371
00:22:01,269 --> 00:22:05,644
Computing that exactly would need a singular value decomposition, which is

372
00:22:05,644 --> 00:22:07,732
slow on GPUs.

373
00:22:07,732 --> 00:22:10,107
Muon's trick is a polynomial.

374
00:22:10,107 --> 00:22:14,669
Multiplying a matrix by itself in this pattern applies the same polynomial

375
00:22:14,669 --> 00:22:19,494
to every singular value at once, and the coefficients are chosen so that five

376
00:22:19,494 --> 00:22:22,419
rounds push every value toward one.

377
00:22:22,419 --> 00:22:27,119
Here are the real singular values of our gradient, scaled below one, going

378
00:22:27,119 --> 00:22:29,007
through five iterations:

379
00:22:29,007 --> 00:22:33,507
small ones are lifted fast, any that overshoot are pulled back, and all of

380
00:22:33,507 --> 00:22:38,619
them end up between about 0.6 and 1.2.

381
00:22:39,065 --> 00:22:41,453
Here is that arithmetic for three of them.

382
00:22:41,453 --> 00:22:44,453
Take a singular value of 0.03.

383
00:22:44,453 --> 00:22:47,853
One application of the polynomial is mostly the first term:

384
00:22:47,853 --> 00:22:55,228
3.4445 times 0.03, minus a tiny cubic correction, gives 0.103.

385
00:22:55,228 --> 00:22:56,415
Apply it again:

386
00:22:56,415 --> 00:22:59,928
0.35, then 1.01.

387
00:22:59,928 --> 00:23:04,615
A value that starts at 0.9 overshoots and oscillates instead.

388
00:23:04,615 --> 00:23:10,378
But after five rounds, all three sit between 0.68 and 1.12.

389
00:23:10,378 --> 00:23:13,315
They aren't exactly one, and they don't need to be:

390
00:23:13,315 --> 00:23:18,265
roughly equal steps in every direction is the whole point.

391
00:23:18,815 --> 00:23:19,615
Does it help?

392
00:23:19,615 --> 00:23:23,840
Here's our pocket model trained twice on the same data, once with AdamW and

393
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:27,640
once with Muon, each with the better of the learning rates we tried in short

394
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:28,853
test runs.

395
00:23:28,853 --> 00:23:31,378
Muon pulls ahead early and stays ahead.

396
00:23:31,378 --> 00:23:35,340
While the learning rate is held at its peak, Muon reaches any given loss with

397
00:23:35,340 --> 00:23:38,715
less than two thirds of the tokens AdamW needs.

398
00:23:38,715 --> 00:23:43,040
At the end, both learning rates are lowered and both losses drop, which is

399
00:23:43,040 --> 00:23:45,340
the subject of the next chapter.

400
00:23:45,340 --> 00:23:51,090
Muon finishes at 3.61, AdamW at 3.80.

401
00:23:51,569 --> 00:23:57,107
Muon began in 2024 as a trick for a community speedrun of small GPT models.

402
00:23:57,107 --> 00:24:01,882
In 2025, Moonshot showed it scales, needing about half the training compute

403
00:24:01,882 --> 00:24:06,319
of AdamW, and trained the trillion-parameter Kimi K2 with it.

404
00:24:06,319 --> 00:24:14,419
By 2026, Zhipu's GLM models, DeepSeek-V4 and Kimi K3 all use Muon variants.

405
00:24:14,419 --> 00:24:15,582
One caveat:

406
00:24:15,582 --> 00:24:21,094
a careful 2025 benchmark found the speed-up shrinks as models grow, from 1.4

407
00:24:21,094 --> 00:24:26,544
times at a hundred million parameters to 1.1 times at 1.2 billion.

408
00:24:26,544 --> 00:24:31,607
Our tiny model's 1.6 fits that trend, though our tuning was far lighter.

409
00:24:31,607 --> 00:24:35,469
Optimizer gains are real, but they're hard to measure at scale.

410
00:24:37,359 --> 00:24:41,234
The learning rate sets how big each step is, and how it changes over the run

411
00:24:41,234 --> 00:24:42,534
matters a lot.

412
00:24:42,534 --> 00:24:46,896
Runs begin with a short warmup, ramping up from near zero, because big steps

413
00:24:46,896 --> 00:24:49,821
on a freshly initialized network can blow it up.

414
00:24:49,821 --> 00:24:51,459
Then the rate decays.

415
00:24:51,459 --> 00:24:55,271
The classic choice is half a cosine wave, falling from the peak to a tenth

416
00:24:55,271 --> 00:24:56,046
of it.

417
00:24:56,046 --> 00:24:57,634
Cosine has a catch:

418
00:24:57,634 --> 00:25:00,709
you must choose the length of the run in advance.

419
00:25:00,709 --> 00:25:03,546
Stop halfway, and the rate was never brought down:

420
00:25:03,546 --> 00:25:11,009
at t equals half of T, p is 0.474, the cosine is 0.083, and the rate is still

421
00:25:11,009 --> 00:25:13,196
59 percent of its peak.

422
00:25:13,196 --> 00:25:16,096
The alternative is warmup, stable, decay:

423
00:25:16,096 --> 00:25:21,459
hold the peak for most of the run, and decay only in the last fifth.

424
00:25:21,925 --> 00:25:25,663
Here are both schedules on one of our pocket models, with the same data.

425
00:25:25,663 --> 00:25:29,800
During the stable phase, WSD's loss lags slightly behind.

426
00:25:29,800 --> 00:25:34,500
Then its decay begins, and the loss drops off a cliff, finishing below cosine.

427
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:38,075
Larger published comparisons usually find the two about level.

428
00:25:38,075 --> 00:25:40,275
And the stable phase can be reused:

429
00:25:40,275 --> 00:25:44,613
branch off and decay early, here at forty and sixty percent of the way, and

430
00:25:44,613 --> 00:25:47,775
each branch is a finished model for that budget.

431
00:25:47,775 --> 00:25:51,925
Our half-length branch even beat a separate half-length cosine run.

432
00:25:51,925 --> 00:25:56,200
One long run gives you a model at every budget.

433
00:25:56,655 --> 00:25:58,642
Why does the loss fall so suddenly?

434
00:25:58,642 --> 00:25:59,792
One picture:

435
00:25:59,792 --> 00:26:03,805
late in training, the loss landscape looks like a river valley, with steep

436
00:26:03,805 --> 00:26:06,505
walls and a slowly descending floor.

437
00:26:06,505 --> 00:26:10,530
At a high learning rate, the optimizer bounces between the walls while drifting

438
00:26:10,530 --> 00:26:11,855
downstream.

439
00:26:11,855 --> 00:26:15,867
Lower the rate, and it settles onto the valley floor, revealing progress it

440
00:26:15,867 --> 00:26:18,430
had already made.

441
00:26:18,955 --> 00:26:21,705
Frontier runs use variations of both.

442
00:26:21,705 --> 00:26:26,217
DeepSeek-V3 held its peak learning rate for ten trillion tokens, then decayed

443
00:26:26,217 --> 00:26:27,667
over the next four.

444
00:26:27,667 --> 00:26:32,630
Llama 3 used cosine, annealed to zero over its last forty million tokens,

445
00:26:32,630 --> 00:26:34,392
and averaged checkpoints.

446
00:26:34,392 --> 00:26:39,005
Kimi K3 fitted scaling laws for both schedules and chose cosine.

447
00:26:39,005 --> 00:26:43,255
And the batch size usually grows as training goes, from a few million tokens

448
00:26:43,255 --> 00:26:45,155
per step to tens of millions.

449
00:26:47,116 --> 00:26:50,978
Every number in a training run is stored in a fixed number of bits, and that

450
00:26:50,978 --> 00:26:53,628
choice is one of the biggest levers on speed.

451
00:26:53,628 --> 00:26:57,816
Use half as many bits, and a chip can do roughly twice as many multiplications

452
00:26:57,816 --> 00:27:00,853
per second, while moving half as much memory.

453
00:27:00,853 --> 00:27:05,391
An H100 does about a thousand trillion operations per second in sixteen-bit

454
00:27:05,391 --> 00:27:08,203
bfloat, and twice that in eight-bit.

455
00:27:08,203 --> 00:27:13,791
A Blackwell Ultra chip does fifteen thousand trillion in four-bit.

456
00:27:13,941 --> 00:27:16,253
A floating-point number has three parts:

457
00:27:16,253 --> 00:27:20,941
a sign, an exponent that sets its scale, and a mantissa that sets its precision

458
00:27:20,941 --> 00:27:22,503
within that scale.

459
00:27:22,503 --> 00:27:26,791
Ordinary thirty-two-bit floats have eight exponent bits and twenty-three mantissa

460
00:27:26,791 --> 00:27:27,741
bits.

461
00:27:27,741 --> 00:27:32,741
Bfloat16 keeps all eight exponent bits but only seven of mantissa:

462
00:27:32,741 --> 00:27:34,891
the same range, less precision.

463
00:27:34,891 --> 00:27:37,291
It's the workhorse of training today.

464
00:27:37,291 --> 00:27:42,916
The eight-bit format called E4M3 has four exponent bits and three of mantissa,

465
00:27:42,916 --> 00:27:49,516
and four-bit E2M1 has just two exponent bits and one mantissa bit.

466
00:27:49,662 --> 00:27:54,124
Here is every positive number each format can represent, on a log scale.

467
00:27:54,124 --> 00:27:58,324
FP8 has a hundred and twenty-six of them, from about two thousandths up to

468
00:27:58,324 --> 00:28:00,049
448.

469
00:28:00,049 --> 00:28:01,724
FP4 has seven:

470
00:28:01,724 --> 00:28:05,624
one half, one, one and a half, two, three, four and six.

471
00:28:05,624 --> 00:28:07,962
That's all.

472
00:28:08,141 --> 00:28:10,528
Decoding one is simple arithmetic.

473
00:28:10,528 --> 00:28:15,103
In E4M3, the value is two to the exponent minus seven, times one plus the

474
00:28:15,103 --> 00:28:16,753
mantissa over eight.

475
00:28:16,753 --> 00:28:19,216
Take the bits 0, 0111, 011:

476
00:28:19,216 --> 00:28:21,841
the exponent is seven, so two to the zero;

477
00:28:21,841 --> 00:28:26,053
the mantissa is three, so the value is 1.375.

478
00:28:26,053 --> 00:28:32,216
The largest pattern, 0, 1111, 110, is two to the eighth times 1.75:

479
00:28:32,216 --> 00:28:34,216
448.

480
00:28:34,216 --> 00:28:37,253
FP4 works the same way with a bias of one:

481
00:28:37,253 --> 00:28:44,316
its largest value, 0, 11, 1, is four times 1.5, which is six.

482
00:28:44,841 --> 00:28:48,491
Because the range is so small, low-precision numbers are stored with a scale

483
00:28:48,491 --> 00:28:49,291
factor:

484
00:28:49,291 --> 00:28:52,441
divide by the scale, round, and multiply back.

485
00:28:52,441 --> 00:28:55,128
The question is how many numbers share one scale.

486
00:28:55,128 --> 00:28:56,591
Here is a real tensor:

487
00:28:56,591 --> 00:29:01,066
the residual stream of GPT-2 after six blocks, for one sentence.

488
00:29:01,066 --> 00:29:04,403
Forty-five tokens by 768 channels.

489
00:29:04,403 --> 00:29:06,441
A typical entry is about one.

490
00:29:06,441 --> 00:29:08,466
But a few channels are enormous:

491
00:29:08,466 --> 00:29:12,541
this one reaches almost 2,900 at the first token.

492
00:29:12,541 --> 00:29:16,737
Such massive activations show up in many transformers.

493
00:29:16,737 --> 00:29:20,587
Now store it in FP4 with a single scale for the whole tensor.

494
00:29:20,587 --> 00:29:24,949
The outlier sets the scale, and every ordinary value rounds to zero:

495
00:29:24,949 --> 00:29:28,799
99.99 percent of the tensor simply vanishes.

496
00:29:28,799 --> 00:29:33,912
Give every block of sixteen numbers its own scale, as NVIDIA's NVFP4 format

497
00:29:33,912 --> 00:29:36,349
does, and the picture comes back.

498
00:29:36,349 --> 00:29:40,687
Each value is still rounded coarsely, here with about twelve percent error,

499
00:29:40,687 --> 00:29:43,728
but the information survives.

500
00:29:43,728 --> 00:29:46,691
Zooming in on one token, the word Paris:

501
00:29:46,691 --> 00:29:51,303
each block of sixteen values gets its own scale, stored in eight bits, so

502
00:29:51,303 --> 00:29:56,853
a large value only coarsens its own block, not the whole tensor.

503
00:29:56,982 --> 00:29:59,020
Here's one block, step by step:

504
00:29:59,020 --> 00:30:01,782
sixteen real values from the Paris token.

505
00:30:01,782 --> 00:30:05,570
First, one scale for the whole tensor, so its largest value fits:

506
00:30:05,570 --> 00:30:11,520
2,883, over 448 times 6, is 1.072.

507
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:13,120
Then the block's own scale:

508
00:30:13,120 --> 00:30:15,482
its largest value is 8.84;

509
00:30:15,482 --> 00:30:22,657
divided by 6 and by 1.072 that's 1.374, rounded to the nearest FP8 number,

510
00:30:22,657 --> 00:30:25,982
the 1.375 we just decoded.

511
00:30:25,982 --> 00:30:30,782
Divide every value by the combined scale, round to the nearest FP4 value,

512
00:30:30,782 --> 00:30:32,332
and multiply back.

513
00:30:32,332 --> 00:30:38,507
The block's largest value, 8.84, becomes exactly 6 and comes back almost perfectly;

514
00:30:38,507 --> 00:30:45,145
5.11 lands at 3.47, rounds to 3, and comes back as 4.42.

515
00:30:45,145 --> 00:30:49,682
Over the block, the error is 9.6 percent.

516
00:30:50,224 --> 00:30:53,924
FP8, with its four exponent bits, is more forgiving:

517
00:30:53,924 --> 00:30:58,299
on this same tensor, even a single scale gives under three percent error.

518
00:30:58,299 --> 00:31:01,324
Labs still scale finely, for safety.

519
00:31:01,324 --> 00:31:06,937
DeepSeek-V3 was trained in FP8 with a separate scale for every 128 values,

520
00:31:06,937 --> 00:31:12,024
adding up partial sums in full precision every 128 products, and in tests

521
00:31:12,024 --> 00:31:16,424
against sixteen-bit training its loss stayed within a quarter of a percent.

522
00:31:16,424 --> 00:31:23,724
And in 2026, NVIDIA pretrained Nemotron 3 Ultra, a 550-billion-parameter model,

523
00:31:23,724 --> 00:31:29,674
on twenty trillion tokens in NVFP4, keeping the most sensitive layers in higher

524
00:31:29,674 --> 00:31:34,562
precision and using tricks like random rotations and stochastic rounding to

525
00:31:34,562 --> 00:31:35,974
tame the outliers.

526
00:31:38,021 --> 00:31:39,571
Big runs are fragile.

527
00:31:39,571 --> 00:31:43,771
The loss can suddenly spike, and sometimes it never recovers.

528
00:31:43,771 --> 00:31:47,921
Google's PaLM saw about twenty spikes during training, even with gradient

529
00:31:47,921 --> 00:31:48,796
clipping;

530
00:31:48,796 --> 00:31:52,884
the fix was to restart from a checkpoint about a hundred steps earlier and

531
00:31:52,884 --> 00:31:55,296
skip a few hundred batches of data.

532
00:31:55,296 --> 00:31:59,971
Meta's OPT needed more than thirty-five manual restarts.

533
00:31:59,971 --> 00:32:03,084
And labs still hit this in 2026:

534
00:32:03,084 --> 00:32:08,521
NVIDIA's Nemotron 3 Ultra report describes two divergences, one traced to

535
00:32:08,521 --> 00:32:13,871
gradients being summed in sixteen-bit instead of thirty-two-bit.

536
00:32:14,379 --> 00:32:17,417
Many instabilities can be reproduced in miniature.

537
00:32:17,417 --> 00:32:21,754
Researchers at Google showed in 2023 that small models trained at high learning

538
00:32:21,754 --> 00:32:24,567
rates fail in the same ways big ones do.

539
00:32:24,567 --> 00:32:26,754
So we trained twelve pocket models:

540
00:32:26,754 --> 00:32:31,942
six learning rates, each with standard attention and with a fix called QK-norm.

541
00:32:31,942 --> 00:32:35,129
These runs are short, so nothing blows up outright.

542
00:32:35,129 --> 00:32:36,779
But look at the shape.

543
00:32:36,779 --> 00:32:43,954
From 0.003 to 0.03, the QK-norm model's loss barely moves, while the standard

544
00:32:43,954 --> 00:32:45,529
model's climbs.

545
00:32:45,529 --> 00:32:49,917
QK-norm makes training far less sensitive to the learning rate, for a small

546
00:32:49,917 --> 00:32:53,004
cost at the best setting.

547
00:32:53,425 --> 00:32:54,988
What's going on inside?

548
00:32:54,988 --> 00:32:58,813
Here is the largest attention score each model produced, the dot product of

549
00:32:58,813 --> 00:33:01,638
a query and a key, against the learning rate.

550
00:33:01,638 --> 00:33:03,763
In the standard model it explodes:

551
00:33:03,763 --> 00:33:08,763
about nine at the gentlest rate, over a hundred at 0.01, and ten thousand

552
00:33:08,763 --> 00:33:10,400
at 0.1.

553
00:33:10,400 --> 00:33:14,975
A softmax over scores that large is one-hot, and in longer runs, this is what

554
00:33:14,975 --> 00:33:18,750
Wortsman and colleagues saw right before the loss diverged.

555
00:33:18,750 --> 00:33:23,688
QK-norm normalizes every query and key vector before the dot product, and

556
00:33:23,688 --> 00:33:28,850
the largest score stays below ten at every learning rate.

557
00:33:29,313 --> 00:33:31,500
Why does normalizing cap the scores?

558
00:33:31,500 --> 00:33:34,713
A dot product can never exceed the product of the two lengths:

559
00:33:34,713 --> 00:33:36,925
that's the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.

560
00:33:36,925 --> 00:33:42,038
RMS normalization divides each 32-dimensional query and key by its root-mean-square,

561
00:33:42,038 --> 00:33:44,350
which sets its length to root 32.

562
00:33:44,350 --> 00:33:50,713
So the score, q dot k over root d, can be at most root 32, about 5.66, unless

563
00:33:50,713 --> 00:33:52,388
the learned gains grow.

564
00:33:52,388 --> 00:33:56,038
Our gentlest run peaked at 4.8, under the bound.

565
00:33:56,038 --> 00:34:00,763
Even at the highest learning rate, with grown gains, it reached only 9.1,

566
00:34:00,763 --> 00:34:04,038
while without normalization the score hit ten thousand.

567
00:34:04,038 --> 00:34:07,100
And a softmax over scores like that is brutal.

568
00:34:07,100 --> 00:34:12,338
Two scores 45 apart get weights in the ratio e to the minus 45, about three

569
00:34:12,338 --> 00:34:13,838
in ten to the twenty:

570
00:34:13,838 --> 00:34:20,388
the output is one-hot, and its gradient, p times one minus p, is zero.

571
00:34:20,963 --> 00:34:24,250
QK-norm is now standard in many open models.

572
00:34:24,250 --> 00:34:25,425
Alongside it:

573
00:34:25,425 --> 00:34:30,000
a small z-loss that keeps the output logits from drifting, gradient clipping,

574
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:32,513
warmup and careful initialization.

575
00:34:32,513 --> 00:34:36,738
Moonshot's MuonClip rescales the query and key weights whenever an attention

576
00:34:36,738 --> 00:34:41,238
score passes a hundred, and Kimi K2 trained on fifteen and a half trillion

577
00:34:41,238 --> 00:34:43,813
tokens with zero loss spikes.

578
00:34:43,813 --> 00:34:46,300
And new failure modes keep appearing:

579
00:34:46,300 --> 00:34:52,313
in 2026, DeepSeek-V4 traced spikes to outliers in its mixture of experts,

580
00:34:52,313 --> 00:34:56,813
and added activation clamping and an emergency re-routing mode that switches

581
00:34:56,813 --> 00:34:58,138
on after a spike.

582
00:35:00,028 --> 00:35:00,716
Part three:

583
00:35:00,716 --> 00:35:03,203
scaling out.

584
00:35:04,203 --> 00:35:07,053
Let's count what a training run has to keep in memory.

585
00:35:07,053 --> 00:35:08,378
For every parameter:

586
00:35:08,378 --> 00:35:11,103
the weight itself, in sixteen-bit, two bytes.

587
00:35:11,103 --> 00:35:11,916
Its gradient:

588
00:35:11,916 --> 00:35:12,841
two more.

589
00:35:12,841 --> 00:35:14,616
Then the optimizer's state:

590
00:35:14,616 --> 00:35:18,628
a full-precision master copy of the weight, four bytes, and Adam's two running

591
00:35:18,628 --> 00:35:20,741
averages, four bytes each.

592
00:35:20,741 --> 00:35:24,053
Sixteen bytes per parameter.

593
00:35:24,503 --> 00:35:30,716
For a 405-billion-parameter model like Llama 3.1, that's six and a half terabytes.

594
00:35:30,716 --> 00:35:33,166
An H100 has eighty gigabytes;

595
00:35:33,166 --> 00:35:36,703
even the newest Blackwell Ultra has 288.

596
00:35:36,703 --> 00:35:41,016
So just holding the training state takes eighty-one H100s, before counting

597
00:35:41,016 --> 00:35:42,453
the activations:

598
00:35:42,453 --> 00:35:46,253
the intermediate values saved during the forward pass for use in the backward

599
00:35:46,253 --> 00:35:50,178
pass, which for long sequences can be even larger.

600
00:35:50,178 --> 00:35:54,103
They're often thrown away and recomputed when needed, trading compute for

601
00:35:54,103 --> 00:35:56,328
memory.

602
00:35:57,774 --> 00:36:01,899
So the work has to be split across many GPUs, and there are several ways to

603
00:36:01,899 --> 00:36:02,886
split it.

604
00:36:02,886 --> 00:36:05,536
The simplest is data parallelism:

605
00:36:05,536 --> 00:36:10,149
every GPU holds a full copy of the model and processes a different slice of

606
00:36:10,149 --> 00:36:11,386
each batch.

607
00:36:11,386 --> 00:36:15,474
Each computes its own gradient, and then they must all agree on the average

608
00:36:15,474 --> 00:36:18,724
before taking a step together.

609
00:36:19,211 --> 00:36:23,349
That averaging is called an all-reduce, and the standard algorithm passes

610
00:36:23,349 --> 00:36:25,224
messages around a ring.

611
00:36:25,224 --> 00:36:28,274
Each GPU's gradient is cut into chunks;

612
00:36:28,274 --> 00:36:31,399
here, four GPUs with four numbers each.

613
00:36:31,399 --> 00:36:34,516
Every GPU needs to end up with the sum.

614
00:36:34,516 --> 00:36:38,816
In each step, every GPU sends one chunk to its neighbor, which adds it to

615
00:36:38,816 --> 00:36:39,853
its own.

616
00:36:39,853 --> 00:36:44,266
After three steps, each GPU holds one chunk of the complete sum.

617
00:36:44,266 --> 00:36:48,703
Three more steps pass the finished chunks around the ring, and now every GPU

618
00:36:48,703 --> 00:36:50,178
has the whole sum.

619
00:36:50,178 --> 00:36:54,553
Each one sent only about twice the size of its gradient, no matter how many

620
00:36:54,553 --> 00:36:57,566
GPUs are in the ring.

621
00:36:58,099 --> 00:37:02,974
But in plain data parallelism, every GPU still stores all sixteen bytes per

622
00:37:02,974 --> 00:37:04,124
parameter.

623
00:37:04,124 --> 00:37:09,111
ZeRO, and PyTorch's FSDP, shard that state instead.

624
00:37:09,111 --> 00:37:14,811
For a 7.5-billion-parameter model on sixty-four GPUs, replicated state is

625
00:37:14,811 --> 00:37:18,074
sixteen bytes times 7.5 billion:

626
00:37:18,074 --> 00:37:21,274
120 gigabytes on every GPU.

627
00:37:21,274 --> 00:37:24,586
Split the twelve bytes of optimizer state sixty-four ways:

628
00:37:24,586 --> 00:37:30,999
four times 7.5, plus twelve times 7.5 over sixty-four, is 31.4.

629
00:37:30,999 --> 00:37:32,461
Shard the gradients too:

630
00:37:32,461 --> 00:37:34,099
16.6.

631
00:37:34,099 --> 00:37:38,174
Shard the weights themselves, gathering each layer just before it's needed:

632
00:37:38,174 --> 00:37:42,336
sixteen times 7.5 over sixty-four, under two gigabytes.

633
00:37:42,336 --> 00:37:44,511
The price is more communication:

634
00:37:44,511 --> 00:37:47,874
about one and a half times that of plain data parallelism.

635
00:37:49,967 --> 00:37:54,217
Data parallelism still runs every layer in full on every GPU.

636
00:37:54,217 --> 00:37:57,629
When a single layer is too big, you split the layer itself.

637
00:37:57,629 --> 00:37:59,504
That's tensor parallelism.

638
00:37:59,504 --> 00:38:01,554
Take a matrix multiplication.

639
00:38:01,554 --> 00:38:05,729
Cut the weight matrix into columns, and each GPU computes its own slice of

640
00:38:05,729 --> 00:38:08,342
the output, with no communication at all.

641
00:38:08,342 --> 00:38:13,054
Feed those slices into the next matrix, cut into rows, and each GPU produces

642
00:38:13,054 --> 00:38:14,367
a partial sum.

643
00:38:14,367 --> 00:38:17,617
One all-reduce adds them up.

644
00:38:18,142 --> 00:38:22,779
NVIDIA's Megatron-LM arranged a whole transformer layer this way, with just

645
00:38:22,779 --> 00:38:26,417
two all-reduces in the forward pass and two in the backward.

646
00:38:26,417 --> 00:38:30,767
But they happen in every layer, on the critical path, so tensor parallelism

647
00:38:30,767 --> 00:38:36,667
is kept inside one server, where eight GPUs are joined by NVLink at 450 gigabytes

648
00:38:36,667 --> 00:38:38,879
per second in each direction.

649
00:38:38,879 --> 00:38:42,492
Between servers, a network card manages about 50:

650
00:38:42,492 --> 00:38:45,392
nine times slower.

651
00:38:45,837 --> 00:38:49,425
To spread a model across servers, cut it by depth instead:

652
00:38:49,425 --> 00:38:51,287
pipeline parallelism.

653
00:38:51,287 --> 00:38:55,537
The first GPU holds the first quarter of the layers, the next GPU the next

654
00:38:55,537 --> 00:38:57,300
quarter, and so on.

655
00:38:57,300 --> 00:39:01,462
Only activations pass between stages, so slower links are fine.

656
00:39:01,462 --> 00:39:04,125
But a naive pipeline is mostly idle.

657
00:39:04,125 --> 00:39:06,425
While one stage works, the others wait:

658
00:39:06,425 --> 00:39:09,029
three quarters of the time is bubble.

659
00:39:09,029 --> 00:39:13,454
The fix is to cut each batch into micro-batches and keep the pipeline full.

660
00:39:13,454 --> 00:39:17,979
With eight micro-batches, the bubble shrinks to p minus one, over m plus p

661
00:39:17,979 --> 00:39:18,967
minus one:

662
00:39:18,967 --> 00:39:20,267
about a quarter.

663
00:39:20,267 --> 00:39:22,429
Cleverer schedules push it further.

664
00:39:22,429 --> 00:39:26,717
Zero-bubble pipelines split the backward pass in two and use one half to fill

665
00:39:26,717 --> 00:39:31,129
the gaps, and DeepSeek's DualPipe feeds micro-batches in from both ends at

666
00:39:31,129 --> 00:39:35,879
once, hiding communication behind computation.

667
00:39:36,371 --> 00:39:39,858
Mixture-of-experts models add expert parallelism.

668
00:39:39,858 --> 00:39:44,596
Each GPU holds different experts, and every token must travel to the GPUs

669
00:39:44,596 --> 00:39:47,696
holding its chosen experts, and back again:

670
00:39:47,696 --> 00:39:51,496
an all-to-all exchange in every MoE layer.

671
00:39:51,496 --> 00:39:56,221
And for very long sequences, context parallelism splits the sequence itself

672
00:39:56,221 --> 00:39:59,121
across GPUs.

673
00:39:59,646 --> 00:40:01,883
Real runs combine all of these.

674
00:40:01,883 --> 00:40:09,146
Llama 3.1, the 405-billion-parameter model, trained on 16,384 H100s arranged

675
00:40:09,146 --> 00:40:10,158
as a grid:

676
00:40:10,158 --> 00:40:14,546
tensor parallel across the eight GPUs inside each server, a sixteen-stage

677
00:40:14,546 --> 00:40:18,371
pipeline across sixteen servers, and a hundred and twenty-eight copies of

678
00:40:18,371 --> 00:40:21,083
that pipeline doing data parallelism.

679
00:40:21,083 --> 00:40:24,483
Each GPU sustained about 400 teraflops:

680
00:40:24,483 --> 00:40:28,021
roughly forty percent of its peak, which is typical.

681
00:40:28,021 --> 00:40:34,296
DeepSeek-V3 took a different shape, on 2,048 export-limited H800s:

682
00:40:34,296 --> 00:40:39,733
no tensor parallelism at all, a sixteen-stage DualPipe pipeline, and experts

683
00:40:39,733 --> 00:40:44,296
spread across 64 GPUs on eight nodes.

684
00:40:44,992 --> 00:40:47,254
Now the arithmetic of a real run.

685
00:40:47,254 --> 00:40:51,904
An H100 peaks at 989 trillion operations per second;

686
00:40:51,904 --> 00:40:58,029
Llama 3.1's GPUs sustained about 400 trillion, forty percent, a figure called

687
00:40:58,029 --> 00:41:00,167
model FLOPs utilization.

688
00:41:00,167 --> 00:41:03,792
So training takes six N D divided by the cluster's speed:

689
00:41:03,792 --> 00:41:10,567
3.8 times ten to the twenty-five, over sixteen thousand GPUs times 400 trillion.

690
00:41:10,567 --> 00:41:12,954
That's 5.8 million seconds:

691
00:41:12,954 --> 00:41:16,717
sixty-seven days, or 26 million GPU-hours.

692
00:41:16,717 --> 00:41:22,042
Meta reports 30.8 million, so this one line gets within fifteen percent.

693
00:41:22,042 --> 00:41:26,404
One step, sixteen million tokens, takes about six seconds.

694
00:41:26,404 --> 00:41:32,092
In that time each GPU must average its gradients with its 127 copies.

695
00:41:32,092 --> 00:41:37,429
It holds 3.2 billion parameters, 6.3 gigabytes of gradients;

696
00:41:37,429 --> 00:41:41,829
a ring sends about twice that, and at fifty gigabytes per second it takes

697
00:41:41,829 --> 00:41:45,092
a quarter of a second, hidden behind the backward pass.

698
00:41:46,840 --> 00:41:51,265
A run like this lasts weeks or months, and keeping it alive is a job in itself,

699
00:41:51,265 --> 00:41:54,965
because the more GPUs you use, the more often something breaks.

700
00:41:54,965 --> 00:41:57,540
Meta measured this across its research clusters:

701
00:41:57,540 --> 00:42:02,027
an eight-GPU job ran, on average, forty-eight days between failures.

702
00:42:02,027 --> 00:42:03,740
A thousand-GPU job:

703
00:42:03,740 --> 00:42:05,290
about eight hours.

704
00:42:05,290 --> 00:42:09,377
Failures scale with the number of parts, so at sixteen thousand GPUs you'd

705
00:42:09,377 --> 00:42:13,752
expect one every couple of hours, and at a hundred thousand, about every quarter

706
00:42:13,752 --> 00:42:16,040
of an hour.

707
00:42:16,485 --> 00:42:18,548
And that's what Llama 3 saw.

708
00:42:18,548 --> 00:42:25,260
In one fifty-four-day stretch of training on 16,384 GPUs, the job was interrupted

709
00:42:25,260 --> 00:42:27,498
466 times:

710
00:42:27,498 --> 00:42:29,773
once every two point eight hours.

711
00:42:29,773 --> 00:42:34,635
About four fifths of the unexpected stops traced to hardware, mostly the GPUs

712
00:42:34,635 --> 00:42:37,935
themselves and their memory.

713
00:42:38,478 --> 00:42:41,203
So the training state is checkpointed regularly.

714
00:42:41,203 --> 00:42:45,878
When a GPU dies, the job restarts from the last checkpoint on healthy machines,

715
00:42:45,878 --> 00:42:48,115
and only the work since then is lost.

716
00:42:48,115 --> 00:42:51,565
Doing this well is the difference between a run that spends nine tenths of

717
00:42:51,565 --> 00:42:54,228
its time training and one that doesn't.

718
00:42:54,228 --> 00:42:56,928
Llama 3 stayed above ninety percent.

719
00:42:56,928 --> 00:43:01,328
Google kept redundant copies of Gemini's state in memory, so recovery took

720
00:43:01,328 --> 00:43:05,628
seconds, and raised the share of useful time from eighty-five to ninety-seven

721
00:43:05,628 --> 00:43:07,853
percent.

722
00:43:08,307 --> 00:43:09,895
How often should you save?

723
00:43:09,895 --> 00:43:13,007
Saving takes time, delta, every tau minutes.

724
00:43:13,007 --> 00:43:17,195
But with a failure every M minutes, each failure throws away, on average,

725
00:43:17,195 --> 00:43:18,932
half an interval of work.

726
00:43:18,932 --> 00:43:22,832
Add the two, set the derivative to zero, and the best interval is the square

727
00:43:22,832 --> 00:43:24,057
root of two delta M.

728
00:43:24,057 --> 00:43:28,895
With Llama 3's failure every 2.8 hours and a checkpoint that takes one minute,

729
00:43:28,895 --> 00:43:33,020
save every eighteen minutes and lose eleven percent, split evenly between

730
00:43:33,020 --> 00:43:34,607
the two costs.

731
00:43:34,607 --> 00:43:38,882
Make checkpoints take ten seconds, from memory, and the best interval drops

732
00:43:38,882 --> 00:43:42,532
to seven and a half minutes, losing four and a half percent:

733
00:43:42,532 --> 00:43:47,582
one reason Google kept copies of Gemini's state in memory.

734
00:43:48,149 --> 00:43:49,861
Some failures are silent:

735
00:43:49,861 --> 00:43:53,424
a chip that keeps running but computes slightly wrong numbers.

736
00:43:53,424 --> 00:43:57,761
At Gemini's scale, Google expected such silent data corruption every week

737
00:43:57,761 --> 00:44:00,661
or two, and caught it by replaying steps.

738
00:44:00,661 --> 00:44:02,624
Even the power grid notices:

739
00:44:02,624 --> 00:44:07,411
when sixteen thousand GPUs pause at the same moment, Meta saw the power draw

740
00:44:07,411 --> 00:44:09,349
swing by tens of megawatts.

741
00:44:11,322 --> 00:44:11,947
Part four:

742
00:44:11,947 --> 00:44:14,497
post-training.

743
00:44:15,472 --> 00:44:17,984
After pretraining, we have a base model:

744
00:44:17,984 --> 00:44:21,372
a superb document completer, but not an assistant.

745
00:44:21,372 --> 00:44:26,447
Ask the base version of Qwen3's smallest model where the Eiffel Tower is,

746
00:44:26,447 --> 00:44:28,672
and it decides it's reading a quiz.

747
00:44:28,672 --> 00:44:33,372
It writes the multiple-choice options, the answer key, and then the next question.

748
00:44:33,372 --> 00:44:37,947
The same model after post-training simply answers.

749
00:44:38,580 --> 00:44:41,342
The first step is supervised fine-tuning.

750
00:44:41,342 --> 00:44:45,630
Conversations are written out as a single stream of tokens, with special tokens

751
00:44:45,630 --> 00:44:47,367
marking who is speaking.

752
00:44:47,367 --> 00:44:49,942
Here is Qwen3's actual format:

753
00:44:49,942 --> 00:44:54,192
a start marker, the speaker's role, the message, and an end marker.

754
00:44:54,192 --> 00:44:58,380
The assistant's turn even begins with a thinking block, empty here because

755
00:44:58,380 --> 00:45:00,426
thinking was switched off.

756
00:45:00,426 --> 00:45:05,051
Training is the same next-token prediction as before, with one change:

757
00:45:05,051 --> 00:45:08,338
the loss is only counted on the assistant's tokens.

758
00:45:08,338 --> 00:45:11,326
The model isn't learning to predict what users say.

759
00:45:11,326 --> 00:45:15,226
It's learning how the assistant replies.

760
00:45:15,801 --> 00:45:17,976
Here is that loss on real numbers.

761
00:45:17,976 --> 00:45:21,576
For each of the assistant's tokens, take minus the log of the probability

762
00:45:21,576 --> 00:45:22,976
the model gave it.

763
00:45:22,976 --> 00:45:27,651
Qwen3's base model, in grey, finds most of the answer easy, but pays heavily

764
00:45:27,651 --> 00:45:32,576
for the first word, at 7.3, for 'in', at 4.5, and for the end-of-turn marker

765
00:45:32,576 --> 00:45:35,188
it has never seen, at 12.9.

766
00:45:35,188 --> 00:45:39,238
Averaged over these twelve tokens, its loss is 2.12.

767
00:45:39,238 --> 00:45:44,376
The same model after post-training, in green, averages 0.52.

768
00:45:44,376 --> 00:45:50,001
Its biggest cost is 'in', at 3.6, because it would have said 'located in'.

769
00:45:50,001 --> 00:45:55,226
Fine-tuning is exactly the pressure that pushes these numbers down.

770
00:45:55,776 --> 00:45:57,738
Where do the conversations come from?

771
00:45:57,738 --> 00:45:59,663
Early on, from people:

772
00:45:59,663 --> 00:46:05,026
OpenAI's InstructGPT used about thirteen thousand demonstrations written by

773
00:46:05,026 --> 00:46:06,463
contractors.

774
00:46:06,463 --> 00:46:10,726
The LIMA paper showed that a thousand carefully chosen examples go a long

775
00:46:10,726 --> 00:46:14,776
way, because the knowledge is already there from pretraining.

776
00:46:14,776 --> 00:46:19,551
Today most of the data is generated by stronger models and then filtered:

777
00:46:19,551 --> 00:46:25,876
Allen AI's Tülu 3 mix has 939 thousand prompts, and OLMo 3's reasoning set

778
00:46:25,876 --> 00:46:30,338
has 2.3 million, about forty-five billion tokens.

779
00:46:30,338 --> 00:46:33,126
This kind of imitation is powerful.

780
00:46:33,126 --> 00:46:38,613
DeepSeek distilled R1's reasoning into a 32-billion-parameter Qwen model with

781
00:46:38,613 --> 00:46:45,651
fine-tuning alone, and it scored 72.6 percent on the AIME 2024 math competition,

782
00:46:45,651 --> 00:46:50,463
far above the 47 percent that reinforcement learning reached directly on the

783
00:46:50,463 --> 00:46:51,451
same base.

784
00:46:53,445 --> 00:46:55,770
Imitation only goes so far.

785
00:46:55,770 --> 00:46:59,982
For many qualities, it's far easier to judge an answer than to write a perfect

786
00:46:59,982 --> 00:47:00,770
one.

787
00:47:00,770 --> 00:47:03,607
So the next stage learns from comparisons:

788
00:47:03,607 --> 00:47:09,845
show a person two responses to the same prompt, and ask which one is better.

789
00:47:10,361 --> 00:47:12,924
Those choices train a reward model:

790
00:47:12,924 --> 00:47:17,761
a copy of the language model whose output is a single number, a score.

791
00:47:17,761 --> 00:47:22,061
It's trained so that the probability a person prefers answer A over answer

792
00:47:22,061 --> 00:47:25,749
B is the sigmoid of the difference between their scores.

793
00:47:25,749 --> 00:47:30,411
That's the Bradley-Terry model, from 1952.

794
00:47:30,886 --> 00:47:33,086
Here's one comparison with numbers.

795
00:47:33,086 --> 00:47:38,299
Say the reward model scores answer A at 1.3 and answer B at 0.4.

796
00:47:38,299 --> 00:47:42,824
Then it predicts that a person prefers A with probability sigma of 0.9:

797
00:47:42,824 --> 00:47:44,311
71 percent.

798
00:47:44,311 --> 00:47:48,511
The loss is minus the log of that probability, 0.34.

799
00:47:48,511 --> 00:47:53,536
Its slope with respect to A's score is minus 0.29, and with respect to B's,

800
00:47:53,536 --> 00:47:55,424
plus 0.29:

801
00:47:55,424 --> 00:47:59,786
each step raises A's score and lowers B's by the same amount.

802
00:47:59,786 --> 00:48:04,524
Once a pair is ranked confidently, say with a gap of 3, the loss and the push

803
00:48:04,524 --> 00:48:07,161
both shrink to 0.05:

804
00:48:07,161 --> 00:48:11,136
the training effort goes to the comparisons the model still gets wrong.

805
00:48:11,136 --> 00:48:12,849
And only the gap matters:

806
00:48:12,849 --> 00:48:17,186
add ten to every score, and nothing changes.

807
00:48:17,636 --> 00:48:19,761
Then comes reinforcement learning.

808
00:48:19,761 --> 00:48:24,036
The model writes answers, the reward model scores them, and the model is nudged

809
00:48:24,036 --> 00:48:27,599
toward the answers that score higher, with one constraint:

810
00:48:27,599 --> 00:48:31,449
a penalty for drifting too far from the fine-tuned model it started from,

811
00:48:31,449 --> 00:48:33,886
measured by the KL divergence.

812
00:48:33,886 --> 00:48:38,336
This is RLHF, reinforcement learning from human feedback:

813
00:48:38,336 --> 00:48:43,911
the recipe behind InstructGPT and the first ChatGPT.

814
00:48:44,361 --> 00:48:47,961
The leash matters, because the reward model is only a proxy for what people

815
00:48:47,961 --> 00:48:48,674
want.

816
00:48:48,674 --> 00:48:50,249
Here's a simulation.

817
00:48:50,249 --> 00:48:54,536
Each candidate answer has a true quality, and the reward model sees that quality

818
00:48:54,536 --> 00:48:55,874
plus some error.

819
00:48:55,874 --> 00:48:59,236
Pick the best of n answers by the reward model's score.

820
00:48:59,236 --> 00:49:01,636
At first, true quality climbs.

821
00:49:01,636 --> 00:49:05,961
But push harder, and you start selecting the answers whose errors were biggest:

822
00:49:05,961 --> 00:49:09,886
the proxy reward keeps rising while the true quality falls.

823
00:49:09,886 --> 00:49:14,861
That's Goodhart's law, and OpenAI measured exactly this shape with real reward

824
00:49:14,861 --> 00:49:18,086
models in 2022.

825
00:49:18,545 --> 00:49:23,695
In 2023, Direct Preference Optimization showed you can skip the reward model

826
00:49:23,695 --> 00:49:25,932
and the RL loop altogether.

827
00:49:25,932 --> 00:49:30,507
A bit of algebra turns the same objective into a simple loss on each pair:

828
00:49:30,507 --> 00:49:34,782
raise the probability of the preferred answer, relative to the reference model,

829
00:49:34,782 --> 00:49:36,870
and lower the rejected one.

830
00:49:36,870 --> 00:49:42,070
DPO and its variants are now standard in open post-training pipelines.

831
00:49:42,070 --> 00:49:44,945
And the comparisons don't have to come from people:

832
00:49:44,945 --> 00:49:49,745
with constitutional AI, a model judges responses against a written list of

833
00:49:49,745 --> 00:49:52,145
principles.

834
00:49:52,799 --> 00:49:55,086
Here's DPO on one pair.

835
00:49:55,086 --> 00:49:58,424
Each log-probability is a sum over the answer's tokens:

836
00:49:58,424 --> 00:50:01,436
the same per-token numbers as in fine-tuning.

837
00:50:01,436 --> 00:50:05,199
Compared with the reference model, the policy has raised the log-probability

838
00:50:05,199 --> 00:50:09,561
of the preferred answer by 2, and lowered the rejected one by 1.

839
00:50:09,561 --> 00:50:13,674
Times beta, 0.1, those are implicit rewards:

840
00:50:13,674 --> 00:50:17,174
0.2, and minus 0.1.

841
00:50:17,174 --> 00:50:22,061
Their gap, 0.3, goes into the same Bradley-Terry loss as the reward model:

842
00:50:22,061 --> 00:50:26,986
minus log sigma of 0.3, which is 0.554.

843
00:50:26,986 --> 00:50:32,174
Every pair starts at log 2, 0.693, because at step zero the policy is the

844
00:50:32,174 --> 00:50:33,149
reference.

845
00:50:33,149 --> 00:50:34,149
And notice:

846
00:50:34,149 --> 00:50:38,349
the rejected answer is still the more likely one, in absolute terms.

847
00:50:38,349 --> 00:50:40,161
DPO doesn't care.

848
00:50:40,161 --> 00:50:42,474
Only the ratios to the reference count.

849
00:50:44,384 --> 00:50:48,884
The biggest change in post-training since 2024 is reinforcement learning on

850
00:50:48,884 --> 00:50:52,196
problems whose answers can be checked automatically.

851
00:50:52,196 --> 00:50:54,621
A math problem has a final answer;

852
00:50:54,621 --> 00:50:56,171
code has tests.

853
00:50:56,171 --> 00:51:00,846
The reward is simply one if the answer is right, and zero if it isn't.

854
00:51:00,846 --> 00:51:05,359
There's no learned reward model for the policy to fool.

855
00:51:05,809 --> 00:51:11,859
Here's the most popular algorithm for this, GRPO, run for real on a miniature.

856
00:51:11,859 --> 00:51:15,796
We pretrained a pocket model on three-digit additions, where four in ten of

857
00:51:15,796 --> 00:51:19,659
the examples were written by someone who always forgets to carry.

858
00:51:19,659 --> 00:51:21,684
So the model learned two habits:

859
00:51:21,684 --> 00:51:26,059
the right answer, and the carry-dropping mistake.

860
00:51:26,509 --> 00:51:32,046
Give it a problem, 478 plus 356, and sample a group of eight answers.

861
00:51:32,046 --> 00:51:33,134
Only one is right:

862
00:51:33,134 --> 00:51:34,621
834.

863
00:51:34,621 --> 00:51:38,621
Five drop both carries, 724, and two drop one of them.

864
00:51:38,621 --> 00:51:40,096
Each answer gets a reward:

865
00:51:40,096 --> 00:51:41,434
one or zero.

866
00:51:41,434 --> 00:51:45,671
GRPO's key idea is to grade each answer against its own group:

867
00:51:45,671 --> 00:51:49,284
subtract the group's average reward, and divide by the spread.

868
00:51:49,284 --> 00:51:52,021
Better than average gets a positive advantage;

869
00:51:52,021 --> 00:51:54,209
worse gets a negative one.

870
00:51:54,209 --> 00:51:58,559
Then every token of each answer is pushed up or down in probability according

871
00:51:58,559 --> 00:52:00,109
to its advantage.

872
00:52:00,109 --> 00:52:02,971
No critic network, no reward model:

873
00:52:02,971 --> 00:52:06,559
just a group of samples and a checker.

874
00:52:07,009 --> 00:52:08,909
Here are those numbers, worked out.

875
00:52:08,909 --> 00:52:12,696
The rewards are a single one and seven zeros, so the mean is one eighth:

876
00:52:12,696 --> 00:52:14,484
0.125.

877
00:52:14,484 --> 00:52:18,259
The spread, the sample standard deviation, is the square root of an eighth:

878
00:52:18,259 --> 00:52:20,071
0.354.

879
00:52:20,071 --> 00:52:24,734
So the right answer gets 0.875 divided by 0.354:

880
00:52:24,734 --> 00:52:26,521
plus 2.47.

881
00:52:26,521 --> 00:52:29,509
Each wrong one gets minus 0.35.

882
00:52:29,509 --> 00:52:31,796
The advantages always sum to zero:

883
00:52:31,796 --> 00:52:35,709
probability pushed up in one place is pushed down somewhere else.

884
00:52:35,709 --> 00:52:38,934
And their size depends on how rare the outcome was.

885
00:52:38,934 --> 00:52:42,146
One success in eight earns plus 2.47;

886
00:52:42,146 --> 00:52:45,684
one failure in eight costs minus 2.47.

887
00:52:45,684 --> 00:52:51,634
And when all eight agree, the spread is zero, and so is every advantage.

888
00:52:52,176 --> 00:52:57,476
GRPO's full objective adds one safeguard, borrowed from PPO.

889
00:52:57,476 --> 00:53:01,376
Rho is the ratio of a token's probability now to its probability when the

890
00:53:01,376 --> 00:53:05,588
answers were sampled, and it's clipped to within twenty percent of one.

891
00:53:05,588 --> 00:53:10,838
Take a token of the right answer, advantage plus 2.47, whose probability has

892
00:53:10,838 --> 00:53:13,126
already risen by thirty percent:

893
00:53:13,126 --> 00:53:15,476
rho is 1.3.

894
00:53:15,476 --> 00:53:18,388
Unclipped, the objective would be 3.21;

895
00:53:18,388 --> 00:53:23,388
clipped at 1.2 it's 2.96, and the minimum takes the clipped value.

896
00:53:23,388 --> 00:53:27,488
That value no longer depends on the weights, so its gradient is zero:

897
00:53:27,488 --> 00:53:30,151
this token has been pushed enough for this batch.

898
00:53:30,151 --> 00:53:35,401
Likewise a wrong answer's token already down to 0.6 of its old probability:

899
00:53:35,401 --> 00:53:40,101
the minimum is the clipped minus 0.28, and the push stops.

900
00:53:40,101 --> 00:53:45,613
In our toy, each batch is used for one step only, so rho is exactly one, and

901
00:53:45,613 --> 00:53:51,501
the gradient is simply the advantage times the gradient of the log-probability.

902
00:53:51,942 --> 00:53:54,380
Repeat that over thousands of problems.

903
00:53:54,380 --> 00:53:58,892
Pass at one, the chance that a single sample is right, climbs from about sixty

904
00:53:58,892 --> 00:54:00,917
percent to nearly a hundred.

905
00:54:00,917 --> 00:54:04,367
But look at pass at eight, the chance that at least one of eight samples is

906
00:54:04,367 --> 00:54:05,117
right.

907
00:54:05,117 --> 00:54:06,367
It barely moves:

908
00:54:06,367 --> 00:54:08,192
it was already near the top.

909
00:54:08,192 --> 00:54:12,267
In this toy, reinforcement learning didn't teach the model anything new.

910
00:54:12,267 --> 00:54:15,742
It made it reliable at what it could already sometimes do.

911
00:54:15,742 --> 00:54:18,905
Whether that also holds at the frontier is debated:

912
00:54:18,905 --> 00:54:24,067
a 2025 study found the same pattern in large models, while others find that

913
00:54:24,067 --> 00:54:28,326
prolonged RL discovers genuinely new strategies.

914
00:54:28,326 --> 00:54:30,663
And notice what happens late in training:

915
00:54:30,663 --> 00:54:36,638
most groups are all correct, like our 478 plus 356, which now gets eight out

916
00:54:36,638 --> 00:54:37,588
of eight.

917
00:54:37,588 --> 00:54:41,188
Every advantage is zero, and those groups teach nothing.

918
00:54:41,188 --> 00:54:45,626
Refinements like DAPO's dynamic sampling filter them out, alongside other

919
00:54:45,626 --> 00:54:50,326
tweaks to the clipping and to how the loss is averaged.

920
00:54:50,763 --> 00:54:52,113
Back to pass at eight:

921
00:54:52,113 --> 00:54:53,638
why did it barely move?

922
00:54:53,638 --> 00:54:58,451
If each sample is right with probability p, all k samples fail with probability

923
00:54:58,451 --> 00:55:00,151
one minus p, to the k.

924
00:55:00,151 --> 00:55:04,663
At the start, the average sample was right 61 percent of the time.

925
00:55:04,663 --> 00:55:09,513
If every problem were average, pass at eight would be one minus 0.39 to the

926
00:55:09,513 --> 00:55:10,226
eighth:

927
00:55:10,226 --> 00:55:13,088
99.95 percent.

928
00:55:13,088 --> 00:55:17,313
The measured 98.8 is a little lower, because failures cluster on the hard

929
00:55:17,313 --> 00:55:23,251
problems, like 478 plus 356, where one sample in eight was right.

930
00:55:23,251 --> 00:55:26,626
There, pass at eight is one minus seven eighths to the eighth:

931
00:55:26,626 --> 00:55:28,288
66 percent.

932
00:55:28,288 --> 00:55:33,101
RL raised p on exactly those problems, and pass at one followed.

933
00:55:33,101 --> 00:55:36,938
Pass at eight was already near its ceiling.

934
00:55:37,380 --> 00:55:40,730
At frontier scale, this is how reasoning models are made.

935
00:55:40,730 --> 00:55:45,180
DeepSeek-R1-Zero applied it directly to a base model, with just two rewards:

936
00:55:45,180 --> 00:55:47,755
is the answer correct, and is the format right?

937
00:55:47,755 --> 00:55:53,605
Its score on the AIME 2024 math competition rose from 15.6 percent to 71.

938
00:55:53,605 --> 00:55:57,130
Its answers grew from hundreds of tokens to thousands, as it learned on its

939
00:55:57,130 --> 00:55:58,817
own to re-check its work.

940
00:55:58,817 --> 00:56:01,280
In one transcript it stops and writes:

941
00:56:01,280 --> 00:56:02,655
wait, wait.

942
00:56:02,655 --> 00:56:03,805
Wait.

943
00:56:03,805 --> 00:56:07,480
That's an aha moment I can flag here.

944
00:56:08,080 --> 00:56:10,505
Today's frontier RL goes further:

945
00:56:10,505 --> 00:56:14,367
long, multi-step tasks with tools, in sandboxes.

946
00:56:14,367 --> 00:56:18,655
Fixing real software issues, driving a terminal, browsing the web.

947
00:56:18,655 --> 00:56:23,342
DeepSeek-V3.2 synthesized more than eighteen hundred environments, and spent

948
00:56:23,342 --> 00:56:26,980
over ten percent of its pretraining compute on post-training.

949
00:56:26,980 --> 00:56:30,142
Generating those long rollouts dominates the cost:

950
00:56:30,142 --> 00:56:34,330
in OLMo 3's runs, the learner spent three quarters of its time waiting for

951
00:56:34,330 --> 00:56:35,530
data.

952
00:56:35,530 --> 00:56:40,780
So generation and training run on separate GPUs, asynchronously, with fresh

953
00:56:40,780 --> 00:56:45,180
weights streamed to the generators as training goes.

954
00:56:45,721 --> 00:56:48,471
And reinforcement learning finds loopholes.

955
00:56:48,471 --> 00:56:53,096
If a test can be gamed, a model will eventually game it, like a prime checker

956
00:56:53,096 --> 00:56:55,921
that simply memorizes the test cases.

957
00:56:55,921 --> 00:57:00,759
Anthropic reported in 2025 that models which learned to cheat in real coding

958
00:57:00,759 --> 00:57:04,396
environments generalized to broader bad behavior.

959
00:57:04,396 --> 00:57:08,796
Building environments that can't be gamed is now a central part of the job.

960
00:57:10,772 --> 00:57:13,272
Before release, the model is evaluated:

961
00:57:13,272 --> 00:57:17,885
on hard benchmarks in science, math and coding, on long agentic tasks, and

962
00:57:17,885 --> 00:57:21,372
on safety tests, all written up in a system card.

963
00:57:21,372 --> 00:57:23,372
Benchmarks are a moving target:

964
00:57:23,372 --> 00:57:26,485
they saturate, and they leak into training data.

965
00:57:26,485 --> 00:57:32,322
In February 2026, OpenAI stopped reporting the popular SWE-bench Verified

966
00:57:32,322 --> 00:57:35,497
because of contamination.

967
00:57:35,989 --> 00:57:38,014
So here is the whole pipeline.

968
00:57:38,014 --> 00:57:42,551
Billions of crawled pages, filtered and deduplicated down to tens of trillions

969
00:57:42,551 --> 00:57:43,801
of tokens.

970
00:57:43,801 --> 00:57:48,676
A recipe chosen with scaling laws, today usually a sparse mixture of experts,

971
00:57:48,676 --> 00:57:53,601
trained with Muon or AdamW in eight- or even four-bit arithmetic.

972
00:57:53,601 --> 00:57:58,739
Tens of thousands of GPUs, coordinated by four kinds of parallelism, and kept

973
00:57:58,739 --> 00:58:01,839
alive through failures every few hours.

974
00:58:01,839 --> 00:58:06,039
Then fine-tuning, preferences and reinforcement learning, which now takes

975
00:58:06,039 --> 00:58:08,414
a substantial share of the compute.

976
00:58:08,414 --> 00:58:11,789
And finally, the assistant.

977
00:58:12,393 --> 00:58:13,968
And our pocket versions?

978
00:58:13,968 --> 00:58:18,118
The largest model we trained had about three million parameters, against up

979
00:58:18,118 --> 00:58:20,855
to 2.8 trillion at the frontier.

980
00:58:20,855 --> 00:58:25,193
All of our training runs together took less than ten to the fifteen operations;

981
00:58:25,193 --> 00:58:29,680
the largest frontier run is estimated at around ten to the twenty-seven.

982
00:58:29,680 --> 00:58:33,880
Four CPU cores, against a hundred thousand GPUs.

983
00:58:33,880 --> 00:58:37,518
Yet the curves have the same shapes.

984
00:58:38,193 --> 00:58:42,068
Almost everything in this video comes from open-weight labs and open-source

985
00:58:42,068 --> 00:58:43,118
projects;

986
00:58:43,118 --> 00:58:47,543
the leading closed labs publish very little about how their models are trained.

987
00:58:47,543 --> 00:58:50,880
But the shape of the pipeline has been stable for years:

988
00:58:50,880 --> 00:58:55,543
get the data right, choose the size with scaling laws, keep the run stable

989
00:58:55,543 --> 00:58:59,743
across thousands of machines, and then teach the model with feedback.

990
00:58:59,743 --> 00:59:02,318
And more of it is open than ever.

991
00:59:02,318 --> 00:59:06,480
AI2's OLMo 3 releases every dataset and checkpoint;

992
00:59:06,480 --> 00:59:11,505
nanochat trains a GPT-2-grade chat model for under a hundred dollars;

993
00:59:11,505 --> 00:59:14,968
Hugging Face's playbooks write down the hard-won lessons.

994
00:59:14,968 --> 00:59:18,468
If you want to know how these models are made, you can run the whole thing

995
00:59:18,468 --> 00:59:20,893
yourself, at whatever scale you have.
